+
+### 5.7 Bounded directory-parent acquisition and reuse (#190)
+
+`core/crates/layerfs-content/src/filesystem/update.rs` consumes the validated,
+strictly ordered directory-update slice in batches bounded by the smaller of
+`FilesystemResources.base_read_batch` and `MAXIMUM_READ_DEMANDS`. It excludes
+unreachable and declared-new parents from base demands; a build has no base
+lookup. Existing `lookup_many` shares ancestors within each batch. No sorting,
+deduplication, new cache, or format change is needed: input validation already
+proved parent order and uniqueness.
+
+Each acquired immutable-base `InodeValue` supplies both the old directory root
+and the final metadata when the caller omitted a typed value. A supplied value
+still takes precedence. The new content root is overlaid onto that value and
+passed directly to the existing reference reducer. The batch values are then
+discarded. This removes both singleton parent acquisition and the second lookup
+formerly used to recover omitted metadata.
+
+The old operation-sized `contents` map is removed. The later inode-input pass
+uses the existing binary-search `input.update_for` to identify directories
+already processed. Extra retained lookup state is limited to one serial vector
+and one optional-value vector for the current batch, not the whole base tree or
+the complete directory-update set. Validation retains its existing checks and
+its own existing operation-local memo; this change does not extend that memo's
+lifetime or change its trust assumptions.
+
+Directory-value insertion now occurs during the `directories` phase, before
+later directories' binding observations. The reducer keeps typed values and
+binding effects independently and still resolves counts only after all effects.
+This can change spill cadence and the point of an I/O/quota failure; explicit
+ordering quotas and checked cleanup remain enforced. Phase comparisons must
+include this moved bookkeeping and distinguish it from the complete-operation
+total. Read counts and canonical equivalence are verified externally; this
+architecture description makes no latency claim.
